" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+3=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${e}^{2y}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)={e}^{x},b(x)=4,c(x)=0,$ and $f(x)=2+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+5y=x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-2x,c(x)=5,$ and $f(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5376bc4a-d2ee-53b0-8e83-f4f85ee671db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463892} {:answer/id #uuid "e836b290-1712-593e-90f1-85ca64c5b2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5376bc4a-d2ee-53b0-8e83-f4f85ee671db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463892} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61661a3-fd91-5136-a7ad-2a91a6b84524" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf03b04-4823-5f32-bafa-b26f7eff5b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463894} {:answer/id #uuid "f58e7ac7-373e-5c44-b04b-79bdda1f1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f58e7ac7-373e-5c44-b04b-79bdda1f1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463895} :answer-field/id #uuid "734e4fb0-c9f7-5eb8-a761-e27666d89c5a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca0dd39-832e-5666-a234-19e027e0e5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463896} {:answer/id #uuid "d26d49be-9c70-5131-999c-164faa0ba30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d26d49be-9c70-5131-999c-164faa0ba30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d9e8741-b915-5b8c-96a9-8e10a9e3c79e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66d63369-22c5-5a2f-adb6-cc0d49c2dc04" :question/math-academy-id "q-130677" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n- **I.** $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{y}^{′}+{y}^{2}=0$\n- **II.** $2{y}^{″}+{x}^{3}{y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}=x{e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{y}^{′}+{y}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}+{x}^{3}{y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=2,b(x)={x}^{3},c(x)=2x,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}=x{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=x,c(x)=0,$ and $f(x)=x{e}^{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82c68fda-0f42-5c48-9715-68ca5d6ebb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463898} {:answer/id #uuid "ae743fce-b45e-5abc-bd86-752ba9bcbc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae743fce-b45e-5abc-bd86-752ba9bcbc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463899} :answer-field/id #uuid "01448568-f01a-5eb2-9776-853e263f1da6" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b1bf75-980f-5157-a21e-c5aaa7d1d2bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463900} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2a98f4-65b2-5c86-bfcb-8b40e5c7595b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce2a98f4-65b2-5c86-bfcb-8b40e5c7595b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463901} :answer-field/id #uuid "124e1032-53d8-59c4-9767-8aa9b08c5f27" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11393e6b-38fe-5ee8-a898-8839130cc2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463902} {:answer/id #uuid "1d89d028-e268-579c-869b-ea7d5ab042e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11393e6b-38fe-5ee8-a898-8839130cc2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463902} :answer-field/id #uuid "2539df0a-7237-5aec-a2e8-de82405d21b5" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ec5e472-dc1d-5891-bb93-866a6d9b3914" :question/math-academy-id "q-130774" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85584b3a-c6d4-52ce-a49a-aa5715567882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463904} {:answer/id #uuid "cf095273-2cd2-5877-9b70-2ae96d4c801f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85584b3a-c6d4-52ce-a49a-aa5715567882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463904} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb66091b-2fc4-59f6-a708-47550a698f13" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6db556-cb94-59ea-a9e7-0f8969a09752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463906} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4c9764-6730-51df-88b4-124dc4a80da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4c9764-6730-51df-88b4-124dc4a80da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463907} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e3144f-97b9-5d5a-89f4-b7c096a09a55" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bceb62a-d4d0-52cb-8e10-cb9ebc37fa50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463908} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab86023-4843-59bc-9d48-b19698f2895f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bceb62a-d4d0-52cb-8e10-cb9ebc37fa50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463908} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b7e2d5-5d62-5a54-803b-60764628a7a5" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4fc937e-2b7d-5514-a470-2dba13ab48f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130868" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af26650-ec92-5825-8bf7-ee6389246739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc23aaf2-cd82-5980-a662-d5c724c0a3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af26650-ec92-5825-8bf7-ee6389246739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463910} :answer-field/id #uuid "26cc8fec-a525-553b-84c7-2a49f9f7a723" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8050b864-d756-570c-bd55-2d6a32259063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463912} {:answer/id #uuid "15e5f25d-f44f-5e9b-a0ce-a0a2af2176a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e5f25d-f44f-5e9b-a0ce-a0a2af2176a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463913} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf4a2dd-ef1c-5f26-ad2d-413c7be0f7cc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f248fc35-c661-5a21-bea0-6e5c93c92540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463914} {:answer/id #uuid "639ef54f-38f0-5daa-b3e3-33ab6892a11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639ef54f-38f0-5daa-b3e3-33ab6892a11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463915} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b143b8-d1db-5fb6-b810-dd1742976cc5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e2ad896-b5d8-56df-9f2a-960d9cc29ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130997" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}+y=x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1$\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=xy,\\,y(0)=3,\\,y(1)=4$\n- **III.** $2{y}^{″}+{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6072c7e5-1b9e-53ce-b826-0c4a3ff90a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463916} {:answer/id #uuid "12f38159-dc63-5fba-849a-fd4cbddd37a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6072c7e5-1b9e-53ce-b826-0c4a3ff90a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e220417c-1d48-5050-a73f-0084d4f70153" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f55f435-e3f7-5996-93ac-f561dbe5c765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463918} {:answer/id #uuid "b56b3f3f-19de-51b4-b8a9-fc77eab7606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b56b3f3f-19de-51b4-b8a9-fc77eab7606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463919} :answer-field/id #uuid "69952d04-741c-5c07-a22e-2cc948d39226" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3b12fe-cd05-5467-8f10-72a36bc176df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463920} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdfa32f-e041-5523-8c32-8b0bb4b269a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3b12fe-cd05-5467-8f10-72a36bc176df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6309537b-a09e-5bac-8903-1a1e4188fddb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d793473-d655-59e4-94f6-f213bc81d597" :question/math-academy-id "q-131001" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2$\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=1,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=2$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be623d32-4c83-52ca-b054-8ae804533930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463922} {:answer/id #uuid "197b7b8b-1618-51ee-a1d0-602649a1d395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be623d32-4c83-52ca-b054-8ae804533930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463922} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7da93f-871b-59ab-a1ff-a6153daafe12" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4fe36c6-91c9-5794-a277-8e54026a0a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463924} {:answer/id #uuid "484fb7b2-afe2-55df-b44e-37f39f4d84eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "484fb7b2-afe2-55df-b44e-37f39f4d84eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463925} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea33b57-7f98-5328-978c-c61dfdbb78dd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f14051-6183-5864-b2e8-96c6ef2d3590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463926} {:answer/id #uuid "9443aa51-acd9-5ce5-a003-657c72a87b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f14051-6183-5864-b2e8-96c6ef2d3590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463926} :answer-field/id #uuid "2393e142-424a-5c02-bddc-5ad48eec1ba4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bddcd08-8ec0-5aef-80c9-79dc52612a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-131013" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$\n- **II.** $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$\n- **III.** $y(x)={e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16df337d-8576-522d-8836-284d6911bacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463928} {:answer/id #uuid "500ed30b-205a-5c4c-8211-d17d01a58518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16df337d-8576-522d-8836-284d6911bacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463928} :answer-field/id #uuid "60523086-050b-5f92-b28e-ecaa414aceb6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6adb727-0baa-5326-8e66-de05be9f2eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463930} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9202e9-0898-5199-adda-5e295b71acac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6adb727-0baa-5326-8e66-de05be9f2eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463930} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2c9a13-7077-5361-9bf5-4c60e322dc18" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a4e554-a8e4-5d20-be63-024b22e44187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463932} {:answer/id #uuid "18a51eb7-83a7-5210-8fa3-f79f5466c0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a51eb7-83a7-5210-8fa3-f79f5466c0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463933} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e88fe49-bfc9-5250-9ea4-94b933fcaf66" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1f63a2-8822-5dfd-aa6c-f1c9c0adcac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- **II.** $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- **III.** $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f78c56-298b-5849-b85e-66ae8c5979f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463934} {:answer/id #uuid "05e27b72-6ce3-5b06-bdb8-a643f37319c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32f78c56-298b-5849-b85e-66ae8c5979f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463934} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3cf08ac-c38c-5451-a216-7bc713a6faf8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5234c90-ac66-525b-ac97-39c9db5c2b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463936} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb0694b-b963-5e9a-a6ab-ca887d89c228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cb0694b-b963-5e9a-a6ab-ca887d89c228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463937} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b6f350-23a3-5cf7-872e-2fb6e4d91e49" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de652c36-7ad1-5a65-86fb-b7cceb87bf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463938} {:answer/id #uuid "33c401e5-019a-5b4f-bc01-12134fc0618e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de652c36-7ad1-5a65-86fb-b7cceb87bf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463938} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb896600-7a23-5819-9363-f8b7843a99b5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a36da13-eb76-5bc5-81fc-b75234c7173d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131016" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation $4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- **II.** $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0376114-c41b-5238-8817-841eba5d5fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463940} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae21342-124b-5d16-ac26-43ea0aade282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0376114-c41b-5238-8817-841eba5d5fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463940} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c8c015-0913-5d4b-8128-2ce7e9902635" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b29eef7-0dba-5507-bb77-8d6b6d46d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463942} {:answer/id #uuid "7144041a-6509-5887-8f83-1d43e7ff6e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b29eef7-0dba-5507-bb77-8d6b6d46d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463942} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d17a32d-ae79-505d-9bc9-6757a44babdf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464905} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3fa1d4-4501-591a-8efe-d15597443e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463944} {:answer/id #uuid "072a40d8-5171-509c-8c5c-478de2c5e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072a40d8-5171-509c-8c5c-478de2c5e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463945} :answer-field/id #uuid "733e640b-eaa7-5cd7-9bc6-43cfad37e79b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca8233-9802-5cda-93e4-fba4e6ea4800" :question/math-academy-id "q-131019" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=x$\n- **II.** $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}$ $$\n- **III.** $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}=x{e}^{x}({e}^{x})$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.  The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cdd4b7-c024-51bc-adbd-f4a