ion is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2x)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5197832f-c920-574d-8c49-3f63005d0fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463976} {:answer/id #uuid "be0c064d-6c98-5b3b-b65b-a4b7d2be0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be0c064d-6c98-5b3b-b65b-a4b7d2be0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463977} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef8ebd7-dcb5-5f0c-8dd3-9ebcaa801d6a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55479ae3-0885-50d1-8a61-b1a41961dafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463978} {:answer/id #uuid "1a04ebdf-a143-57f0-aee2-905d286d0bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a04ebdf-a143-57f0-aee2-905d286d0bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463979} :answer-field/id #uuid "322c6cde-ce48-5b6e-9672-13ef9fea584c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43426609-8fdd-5c6a-b716-328577cf31ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463980} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbd8e38-da61-5274-9aea-b7b74b961584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43426609-8fdd-5c6a-b716-328577cf31ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463980} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d7e7732-6e4e-5c27-aa37-6eedb112b912" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f88ffa9-46f8-5b9e-b471-a9dfa9a9c40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131069" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[3,5]$\n- **II.** $[1,3]$\n- **III.** $(-1,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ee7c76-0edc-5280-8750-c57be156e6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463982} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7eb98f-c9cc-5ff7-882d-e64690a6b105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04ee7c76-0edc-5280-8750-c57be156e6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463982} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fd8351-5e18-5de5-94d1-03f8c50a37ae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a626c962-824d-5606-985e-f857cd67e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463984} {:answer/id #uuid "d38e8240-5e70-5a8a-b490-77c795b5484d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38e8240-5e70-5a8a-b490-77c795b5484d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463985} :answer-field/id #uuid "269d9c9c-03b3-507a-ae6c-52479928bb8f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a4309a-eba9-5e24-9584-050827d306d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463986} {:answer/id #uuid "86c08b66-dfaf-5856-9af2-f0ab92ff0c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c08b66-dfaf-5856-9af2-f0ab92ff0c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463987} :answer-field/id #uuid "349fc697-8240-52e6-8d87-9502507de6df" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d989e0b-f261-53e6-891f-e36529fecf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-131180" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $[-2,0]$\n- **III.** $[0,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c3c38d0-c5ee-51a5-a634-73869c8a9051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463988} {:answer/id #uuid "a5abb1b5-aa47-59c8-bb04-5bab40e3a464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3c38d0-c5ee-51a5-a634-73869c8a9051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463988} :answer-field/id #uuid "260a60c0-4a86-55b9-b153-b9972312fe23" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9349efc-83ce-557d-a5dd-eaaf0f4b374f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463990} {:answer/id #uuid "500eb1d4-4cd3-5a05-9f47-b1ee5ab4aad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9349efc-83ce-557d-a5dd-eaaf0f4b374f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463990} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f7105d-60c5-5892-a339-4d601a308a96" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be3fd1e-0669-5f34-b527-da1a9b508b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463992} {:answer/id #uuid "903ff4ef-beaf-5981-9576-02f1ee9ebc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be3fd1e-0669-5f34-b527-da1a9b508b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463992} :answer-field/id #uuid "9473659a-cb3e-51ed-8227-bcaa2678cdef" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "675c49ee-f80c-5132-87b4-3f13525060ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-131460" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08130944-12c1-577e-bd10-92c3cc175e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463994} {:answer/id #uuid "0746adc8-d7ef-5ea2-8a27-c0256a25e0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0746adc8-d7ef-5ea2-8a27-c0256a25e0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463995} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dcddf0a-c546-5436-82e3-4430ef6e9e30" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37207318-e098-5102-9d19-017960786590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463996} {:answer/id #uuid "923a703d-2b21-585b-8805-1306fe9e7940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "923a703d-2b21-585b-8805-1306fe9e7940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463997} :answer-field/id #uuid "c970b95f-bd30-5132-a907-13acda7e5049" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca36c4a0-f735-5c25-9e94-781b589915a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463998} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3272d0-9a13-5b65-b05a-4c78acbac552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca36c4a0-f735-5c25-9e94-781b589915a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463998} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a802987-ccf9-5810-b5c8-b5eaf4783d81" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53d0afb1-1358-5254-8618-617a2b1cbc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133250" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "373bcd17-296b-5f60-902f-87e57e7e806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464000} {:answer/id #uuid "506240e8-5111-5d30-9b6e-b7702f6f2c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "506240e8-5111-5d30-9b6e-b7702f6f2c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1b9b74-1b46-5862-8abc-84248f68e9cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464934} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23df4009-37dd-53ff-b0f7-b1d42a2999c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464002} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e90a21-d2ca-5629-bc17-0b1dd7a7bba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e90a21-d2ca-5629-bc17-0b1dd7a7bba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464003} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a01edb-7d3c-59f1-94db-d9d3db00074a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94596486-42b6-51b8-bcc0-8b9aae59584a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464004} {:answer/id #uuid "1d88af83-3945-5cb9-a062-37228bb58bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94596486-42b6-51b8-bcc0-8b9aae59584a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464004} :answer-field/id #uuid "77b8e89d-05eb-5f2f-8a33-9e280f003464" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27d730e5-f1a8-51a8-a220-f1ae1c972c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-133331" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=7x-3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-7y$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
